-- D222 / plan 0.95 A′: the effectful-closure ROUND TRIP — build one with
-- `curry`, eliminate it with `apply`, observe the effect.
--
-- Phase A made a curried effectful closure WRITABLE (`k : Int -> Eff Int Unit`,
-- outer arrow pure, inner eff — the shape Agda's coverage checker named as
-- missing). It could not be ELIMINATED: `t-apply-check`/`t-apply-app-infer`
-- fixed their arrow to `pure`, so `apply` would not take one. That was a dead
-- end introduced by Phase A, and A′'s `t-apply-eff-app-infer` closes it.
--
-- `curry` CAPTURES its first component, so `k 5` closes over 5 and `apply`
-- supplies 9. The companion `apply-eff-closure-snd` selects the OTHER one. The
-- pair is the point: `fst` emits 5 and `snd` emits 9, so a closure that dropped
-- its capture fails the first and an `apply` that dropped its argument fails the
-- second. Neither can pass by accident — which is exactly what the cata-emit
-- tests lacked before D220.
--
-- Expected: exit code 7, trace [5].

import I.Linux.Syscalls as S
import I.Test.Emit as E

-- BUILD: `curry f a b = f (a , b)`, so this emits whichever component `fst`
-- selects — the CAPTURED one.
k : Int -> Eff Int Unit
k = curry (compose emit@E fst)

-- ELIMINATE: `(k 5, 9)` is the pair literal `(Int ⇒[eff] Unit) * Int`.
fire : IO Unit
fire = apply (k 5, 9)

main : IO Unit
main = compose exit@S (compose (\_ -> 7) fire)
